# Select the supported connectivity architecture

## Goal

Choose the adapter strategy and keep authentication on Proton-owned surfaces
without turning browser automation into part of the product security boundary.

## Completed work

- Select Proton Mail's direct QR/manual-code session-fork flow as the supported
  version-one authentication path.
- Use the third-party `Other` child identity rather than a Proton-owned Mail ID.
- Reject Bridge, bundled Playwright/Chromium, browser-profile scraping, and raw
  account-credential login as supported fallbacks.
- Record the current Mail Rust SDK and Drive CLI implementations as evidence for
  target-side fork initiation, payload framing, polling, key transfer, refresh,
  and revocation behavior.
- Specify account-scoped custody for revocable direct-session authority.
- Define the provider-neutral port consumed by runtime composition.
- Define adapter selection, capability negotiation, and failover rules without
  ever combining partial results from different adapters silently.

## Acceptance evidence

- `docs/architecture/connectivity.mdc` accepts direct session-fork authority as
  the only version-one Mail provider path.
- Authentication remains separate from MCP; account passwords, handoff secrets,
  access/refresh tokens, key passwords, and session blobs never cross MCP.
- Current Proton Mail SDK source proves target and host QR-login state machines,
  while the Drive CLI proves the same fork family is usable by third-party
  software without password replay.
- The decision explicitly forbids impersonating Proton's official Mail IDs and
  documents why the current desktop browser route cannot honestly use `Other`.
- `MailProviderPort` and `select_adapter` define the provider-neutral selection
  boundary without dynamic dispatch in the domain.
- Synthetic tests prove that partial adapters are never silently combined and
  that ordered selection requires one complete adapter.
- Targeted domain tests, Clippy, and Jig complete cleanly.
